There is a melon on a stump. It is a very large, very patient melon, and it is, unmistakably, looking at you.
[You are not certain a melon can look. The melon is not certain you can leave.]
A voice comes from somewhere near the melon, mild and rind-deep: "I am waiting. I have been waiting. You took your time."
"Do not pick me up. Picking up is for the unripe. I will know when. So, mercifully, will you."
[The marsh holds its breath. So does the melon. So, you realise, do you.]
"Sit. Wait with me a while. Some things you do not befriend. Some things you simply outlast — together."
